# Full Pyparsing fallback guard v14

The isolated v6 guard completes the changed cold/incremental comparison with
identical serialized state/root and all **67,872 actual blocks**, totaling
**112,728,034 bytes per output**. The independent auditor also compares every
block and state/root to the retained v13 cold output: it is exactly unchanged.
The v13 failed incremental result, all earlier deadlines and original runtime
profiles remain retained.

The complete source inventory is 286 regular files, 154 Python files and one
opaque symlink. The changed source contains 11,701,447 bytes; native graph scope
is 3,957 symbols and 55,859 edges. Nine new native source-read controls pass,
including fresh reads, stale expected state, old bytes, changed bytes and a
between-read source change; the original baseline control remains retained.
No source omissions, provider calls or execution reuse occur.

The guarded incremental scan actually reuses **20 paths, 172 symbols and 1,428
eligible prior frontend edges** and rescans **134 paths**. Whole-inventory graph
resolution still reruns. Projected graph edges are not claimed to be lossless
frontend observations, and the graph edge count is not substituted for the
number of newly extracted original edges.

Both new processes finish within the original 900-second bound. Cold wall time
is **437.699 seconds**, incremental wall time **614.522 seconds**. These workers
ran concurrently on the shared host. Incremental includes **107.173 seconds**
of actual baseline restoration and full native re-verification. Its scan takes
55.736 seconds; the concurrent cold scan takes 52.590 seconds. The original v13
baseline preparation cost of **656.008 seconds** remains paid and recorded.
Native build, verification, guards, persistence and process exit are included
in the worker costs; one-time v14 driver copying/freeze setup was not separately
timed. Nested stage times must not be added again to worker totals.

Seven changing-inventory controls pass under v6 and reproduce the defect under
v5. The unchanged native regression suite records **187 passed, one failed**:
the failed test expects unchanged `mutable.py` to avoid reanalysis, whereas this
correctness fallback intentionally rescans it. The original test and failure
output are preserved. This is a correctness control with a measured reuse
tradeoff, not E01/E03 incremental economy, a token-saving claim or an execution
soundness theorem.

